---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import UberblockRing from "../../components/UberblockRing.astro";
import VisitMap from "../../components/VisitMap.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import HallExhibit from "../../components/HallExhibit.astro";
import Dedication from "../../components/Dedication.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import { gh, OPENZFS_SHA, OPENZFS_TAG } from "../../lib/openzfs";

const lang = "en";
// The Hall in the question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D21.2): the uberblock
// section asks its question, answers it plainly, then the figure, then the code in
// Source notes. Links to the code live in the notes only.
const L = {
  magic: gh("include/sys/uberblock_impl.h", 46),
  slot: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 491, 496),
  mmp: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 488),
  labels: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 560, 562),
  compare: gh("module/zfs/vdev_label.c", 1495, 1505),
  loop: gh("module/zfs/vdev_label.c", 1577, 1585),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="hall"
  title="Uberblok, an interactive museum of ZFS"
  description="An interactive museum of ZFS, room by room: each idea of the OpenZFS source made visible, with the real code."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <p class="kicker">A museum of ZFS, read in its source</p>
        <h1>Uberblok</h1>
        <div class="prose manifesto">
          <p class="lead">
            Uberblok explains how ZFS keeps data safe through crashes, failing disks and interrupted writes. Most rooms take one idea, show it in a figure you can operate, and quotes the OpenZFS code that implements it.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            The figures and excerpts follow OpenZFS <code>{OPENZFS_TAG}</code>, frozen at commit
            <code>{OPENZFS_SHA.slice(0, 12)}</code>. Every excerpt is checked against that commit, and none is written
            from memory.
          </p>
          <p class="why"><a href="#map">All rooms <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a> · <a href="#why">Why this museum? <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-art">
        <HallExhibit lang={lang} />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="object">
      <p class="kicker">The uberblock</p>
      <h2 id="object">How does ZFS find its data after a crash?</h2>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p>
          A pool holds millions of blocks spread over several disks. After a crash, ZFS has to find the last consistent
          state of the pool without reading all of them. It starts from one small record on each disk, the uberblock.
          ZFS writes changes in batches called transaction groups (txg), one every few seconds. The uberblock holds the number of the last txg and a pointer to the top of the pool’s tree of blocks. Every other block can be
          reached from that pointer.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS never writes a new uberblock over the current one. Each disk keeps a ring of slots for uberblocks, and
          each txg writes its uberblock to the next slot. The previous uberblock stays intact in its own slot, so a
          power failure during the write still leaves a good one on disk.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the pool is imported, ZFS reads every slot, sets aside the ones that fail their check, and uses the
          valid uberblock with the highest txg.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose try">
        <p>
          The figure shows one ring. Press Next txg to commit one txg: the new uberblock takes the slot after the previous one, and the previous one stays in its slot. When animations are on, the ring also commits a txg every 1.3 seconds by itself, and Next txg stops it. Change the sector size of the disks. A slot takes one sector, and never less than 1 KiB, so the same 128 KiB ring holds 128 slots
          on disks with 512-byte sectors and 32 on disks with 4 KiB sectors.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <UberblockRing lang={lang} />
      </div>
      <p class="caption aside">
        Every uberblock starts with the same magic number, <code>0x00bab10c</code>, read aloud as “oo-ba-bloc”.
      </p>

      <h3 class="code-title">In the code</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        The figure follows three places in the OpenZFS source. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release <code>{OPENZFS_TAG}</code>.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h4>The record</h4>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>struct uberblock</code> starts with a magic number and the on-disk version. It also holds the txg, the time of the sync and <code>ub_rootbp</code>, the block pointer to the pool’s own metadata, from which the rest of
          the tree hangs.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>struct uberblock</code>, uberblock_impl.h</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="include/sys/uberblock_impl.h"
            from={122}
            to={128}
            symbol="uberblock"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 123,
                text: `<code>UBERBLOCK_MAGIC</code> is <a href="${L.magic}"><code>0x00bab10c</code></a>. A slot without it is not an uberblock.`,
              },
              {
                from: 125,
                text: "The txg of the last sync. On import, the highest valid one wins.",
              },
              {
                from: 128,
                text: "The block pointer to the MOS, the pool’s own metadata. Every other block of the pool is reached from here.",
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h4>The slot of a txg</h4>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>vdev_uberblock_sync</code> picks the slot: the txg modulo the number of slots. The txg grows by one at
          each sync, so the uberblocks go round the ring and the newest one never lands on the one before it.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_uberblock_sync</code>, vdev_label.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/vdev_label.c"
            from={1782}
            to={1784}
            symbol="vdev_uberblock_sync"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1782,
                text: `With <code>multihost=on</code>, the <a href="${L.mmp}">last slot is kept for MMP</a> and the ring turns on one slot fewer.`,
              },
              {
                from: 1783,
                to: 1784,
                text: `<code>VDEV_UBERBLOCK_COUNT</code> is the 128 KiB ring divided by the <a href="${L.slot}">slot size</a>: one sector, at least 1 KiB and at most 8 KiB. The <code>RRSS_SCRATCH_VALID</code> term only matters during a RAID-Z expansion. The same slot is then written in each of the <a href="${L.labels}">four labels</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h4>The choice on import</h4>
        <p class="prose">
          On import, <code>vdev_uberblock_load_done</code> runs once for every slot read. It ignores a slot whose read
          failed or whose magic number is wrong, and keeps the uberblock that compares highest.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_uberblock_load_done</code>, vdev_label.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/vdev_label.c"
            from={1547}
            to={1551}
            symbol="vdev_uberblock_load_done"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1547,
                text: `Every slot of every label of every readable disk <a href="${L.loop}">is read</a>. A slot carries its own checksum, so a damaged one fails the read here.`,
              },
              {
                from: 1549,
                to: 1550,
                text: `<a href="${L.compare}"><code>vdev_uberblock_compare</code></a> compares the txg first, then the timestamp.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />

      <h3 class="code-title">What the figure leaves out</h3>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure shows one ring. A disk carries four labels, two at its start and two at its
        end, each with its own ring, and every txg writes the same slot in all four. The ring is often said to hold 128
        uberblocks. That is true only for 512-byte sectors: it holds 128 KiB, so 64 slots at ashift 11, 32 at ashift 12
        and 16 from ashift 13. With <code>multihost=on</code> one slot is kept for MMP. The txg numbers are made up.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="map">
      <h2 id="map">All rooms</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        Most rooms explain one part of ZFS with a figure and the code behind it. The list starts with the history of ZFS, then follows the data, from the root of the pool to the features built on it. Each room can be read on its own. The last one, the engine
        room, says how this site was made.
      </p>
      <div class="map">
        <VisitMap lang={lang} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <Dedication lang={lang} />
  </div>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="hall" />
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
  }
  /* The release tag and the commit stay whole (zfs-2.4.4 would break at its hyphen). */
  .manifesto code {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* Fig. 1 under the text on a narrow screen, beside it on a wide one (DECISIONS.md D15.2). */
  .hero-art {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .hero {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(26rem, 1fr) minmax(24rem, 34rem);
      grid-template-areas: "kicker art" "title art" "text art";
      grid-template-rows: auto auto 1fr;
      column-gap: var(--space-7);
      align-items: start;
    }
    .hero-text {
      display: contents;
    }
    .kicker {
      grid-area: kicker;
    }
    .hero h1 {
      grid-area: title;
    }
    .manifesto {
      grid-area: text;
    }
    /* The exhibit bleeds into the right margin, up to 6 rem, when there is one. */
    .hero-art {
      grid-area: art;
      margin-top: 0;
      max-width: none;
      margin-inline-end: calc(-1 * min(6rem, max(0px, (100vw - 72rem) / 2)));
    }
  }
  .hero h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-size: clamp(3.2rem, 2rem + 7vw, 7.5rem);
    font-weight: 250;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
  }
  .manifesto {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .why {
    font-family: var(--font-caption);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }
  .why a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .figure {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .aside {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }
  .map {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }
  .answer,
  .try {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }
  .code-title {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }
  .step {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .step h4 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-caption);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    color: var(--accent);
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
</style>
